      <div class="bg-white rounded-2xl border border-gray-200 shadow-sm p-8 md:p-12 prose prose-lg max-w-none">

        <p class="text-xl text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-8">
          Walk down the lock aisle at any home improvement store and you'll find dozens of options. But as a Dover locksmith, we see the same thing repeatedly: homeowners spending money on locks that look solid but offer very little real protection. Here's what actually matters when choosing door locks for your home in Delaware, and which brands and grades we recommend.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Understanding Lock Grades — Start Here</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Before shopping for locks, understand that the American National Standards Institute (ANSI) grades residential locks on a 1–3 scale:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Grade 1</strong> — Commercial/high-security grade. Withstands the most force and picking attempts. Best for front and back doors.</li>
          <li><strong>Grade 2</strong> — Mid-range residential. Good for interior doors and secondary entry points.</li>
          <li><strong>Grade 3</strong> — Basic residential. Most cheap locks you find in big-box stores fall here. Adequate for interior doors only.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          For any exterior door — front door, back door, garage entry — you want ANSI Grade 1. Grade 3 locks are not sufficient for exterior security regardless of how they look.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Why a Deadbolt Is Non-Negotiable</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A doorknob lock alone is not enough security for any exterior door. Doorknob locks use spring latches that can be defeated with a credit card in seconds. Every exterior door in your home should have a properly installed deadbolt as the primary lock.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Key features to look for in a deadbolt:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>1-inch throw bolt</strong> — The bolt should extend at least 1 inch into the door frame.</li>
          <li><strong>Hardened steel bolt</strong> — Resists sawing and cutting attacks.</li>
          <li><strong>Anti-drill protection</strong> — A hardened steel plate or ball bearing inside prevents drilling the cylinder.</li>
          <li><strong>Anti-pick pins</strong> — Security pins in the cylinder make picking much harder.</li>
          <li><strong>Reinforced strike plate</strong> — The door frame is often the weak point. A 3-inch steel strike plate with 3-inch screws dramatically improves kick-in resistance.</li>
        </ul>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          We offer professional <a href="/services/deadbolt-installation/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">deadbolt installation</a> throughout Dover and Kent County, including reinforced strike plates and door frame reinforcement.
        </p>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Top Lock Brands Recommended by Locksmiths</h2>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Schlage — Best Overall Residential Lock</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Schlage is consistently the top recommendation from locksmiths. Their Grade 1 deadbolts (the B60N and B62N series) offer excellent anti-pick, anti-drill, and anti-bump protection at a mid-range price point. Schlage locks are built in the USA and have proven durability in all weather conditions — important for Delaware's climate.
        </p>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The <strong>Schlage B60N</strong> (single cylinder) and <strong>B62N</strong> (double cylinder) deadbolts are workhorses that professional locksmiths trust. They're widely available and we keep them in stock for immediate installation.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Medeco — Best High-Security Lock</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          If you want the best-of-the-best for a front door, Medeco makes some of the highest-security residential locks available. Their patented key control system means keys cannot be duplicated without authorization, and the cylinders are extremely pick and drill resistant. Medeco is the choice for homeowners who want true high-security protection. The price is higher, but so is the peace of mind.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Kwikset SmartKey — Best Value</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Kwikset's SmartKey series offers a convenient re-keying feature — you can rekey the lock yourself without calling a locksmith, using a small tool that comes with the lock. It's a Grade 2 product, so it's not our first choice for front doors, but it's a solid option for secondary entrances or budget-conscious upgrades where self-rekeying convenience is valued.
        </p>

        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-8 mb-3">Smart Locks — Should You Upgrade?</h3>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Smart locks (Schlage Encode, Yale Assure, August Smart Lock) allow keypad or app-based entry and can be convenient for families, Airbnb hosts, or homeowners who frequently need to grant access remotely. A few things to keep in mind:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li>Smart locks are only as secure as the deadbolt they're built on. Look for ANSI Grade 1 smart locks.</li>
          <li>Battery dependency means you need to monitor battery life or you'll be locked out.</li>
          <li>For maximum security, pair a smart lock with a traditional deadbolt backup.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">The Lock Is Only Part of the Security Equation</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          Even the best deadbolt can fail if the door frame isn't reinforced. Most residential break-ins happen by kicking in the door — not picking the lock. The door frame gives way before the lock does. Here's what we recommend for complete door security:
        </p>
        <ul class="list-disc pl-6 text-gray-700 mb-6 space-y-2">
          <li><strong>Door frame reinforcement kit</strong> — Wraps the door frame in steel to prevent kick-in.</li>
          <li><strong>3-inch screws in the strike plate</strong> — Standard 3/4-inch screws barely reach the framing. 3-inch screws anchor into the structural stud.</li>
          <li><strong>Solid wood or metal door</strong> — A hollow-core door negates the value of a good lock entirely.</li>
          <li><strong>Door viewer (peephole)</strong> — Know who's on the other side before opening.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-[#1A1F22] mt-10 mb-4">Professional Installation Makes a Difference</h2>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          A Grade 1 lock installed incorrectly is only as good as a Grade 3 lock. Proper installation means the deadbolt is plumb, the strike plate is fully anchored into the stud, and the bolt engages fully into the strike plate with zero play. Our <a href="/services/residential-locksmith/" class="text-[#A61E22] font-semibold hover:underline">residential locksmith</a> team installs deadbolts throughout Dover, Smyrna, Milford, Harrington, and all of Kent County with guaranteed workmanship.
        </p>

